        <article class="article-card conclusion">
          <p class="eyebrow">Conclusion</p>
          <p>
            This technical sharing was based on my real operational experience in a large-scale cloud network
            environment. It compared traditional networking with cloud networking, analyzed the limitations of the
            existing architecture, and explored the direction of network virtualization.
          </p>
          <p>
            The biggest lesson is that cloud networking is not simply about connecting more servers or adding more
            VLANs. It requires a fundamental shift in architecture.
          </p>
          <p>
            Traditional networks focus on device-level configuration and hop-by-hop forwarding. Cloud networks require
            tenant isolation, flexible service provisioning, software-defined control, network automation, and scalable
            virtualized services.
          </p>
          <p>
            A successful cloud network architecture should minimize operational risk on core devices, provide clear
            resource abstraction, support automated provisioning, and separate tenant-level changes from the physical
            network foundation.
          </p>
          <p>
            This experience gave me a deeper understanding of data center network design, cloud network operations, SDN,
            overlay networking, NFV, and the practical challenges of evolving a traditional carrier-grade network toward
            a cloud-oriented architecture.
          </p>
